cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this framework or build on it, please cite it as below."
title: "A knowledge graph-augmented retrieval framework using Toxics Release Inventory source reduction records to support pollution prevention decisions"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Hernandez-Betancur"
    given-names: "Jose"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2652-1405"
  - family-names: "Ruiz-Mercado"
    given-names: "Gerardo J."
abstract: >-
  Chemical industry stakeholders seek for pollution prevention (P2) strategies while safeguarding
  the technoeconomic feasibility of such process systems engineering endeavours. This requires
  case-by-case design, modeling, testing, and cost optimization of many possibilities for new and
  existing facilities. This study focuses on creating a graph-based learning from-data framework
  utilizing the source reduction activities reported by industrial facilities to the U.S. Toxics
  Release Inventory (TRI) program from 2014 to 2024. For a certain chemical and associated
  industrial and commercial condition of use (CoU), it provides a prioritized list of P2
  initiatives that facilities have previously executed, achieving a cost-effective minimum
  reduction of 25%. The framework employs P2 alternative identification through surrogate
  analysis that integrates chemical structure with industrial sector and CoU information.
  Specifying the CoU made the recommendations more informative, giving engineers a shortlist
  specific to their processes, so feasibility studies can start from options that comparable
  facilities have already proven. In a first case study, the methodology identified supporting
  evidence for eleven chemicals of concern, six via surrogates or alternative uses. In a second
  case study, six chemicals that are not reported to TRI were checked against P2 activities
  documented in peer-reviewed studies; for four of the six, the documented activity was among the
  framework's ten highest-ranked suggestions. The framework transforms established proven
  industrial practices into a foundation for new chemical plant design and retrofits, focusing
  modeling and testing on technically and economically viable alternatives.
repository-code: "https://github.com/jodhernandezbe/tri-chem-graphrag"
